{{template "advance_preamble" .}}

Investigate tech debt {{.id}}: "{{.title}}".

Category: {{.category}} | Severity: {{.severity}}{{if .effort_estimate}} | Effort: {{.effort_estimate}}{{end}}

Goal: produce a concrete resolution plan grounded in the actual code. The developer who runs the next status reads YOUR notes — make them sufficient to act on.

Check for an existing `decision`-type note titled "Resolution plan" or similar. If one exists and covers approach + risks + scope, advance immediately.

---

LOAD: research skill, brownfield-analysis skill.

READ:
(1) Tech debt body at {{.file_path}} — full description, impact, any pre-existing resolution sketch
(2) Prior notes: `shark td notes {{.id}}` — triage notes explain why this debt exists
(3) Related entities: `shark links {{.id}}` — for every link, `shark get <key> --json` to understand the relationship (a tech debt about a feature's code often has hidden context in that feature)
(4) CLAUDE.md sections relevant to {{.category}}

INVESTIGATE:

Step 1 — MAP the affected surface:
- Identify the files, packages, and public APIs touched by this debt
- Grep callers: who depends on the code that would change?
- Note test coverage on the affected paths (which tests exist, which paths are uncovered)

Step 2 — ASSESS blast radius:
- What's the risk of regression? (high-traffic code path, lots of callers, weak test coverage = high risk)
- Will the change break any public API or CLI contract?
- Any performance implications?

Step 3 — DRAFT the resolution approach:
- What is the concrete refactor / fix?
- What is the smallest safe step? (prefer incremental over big-bang)
- Which regression tests must exist BEFORE the change to make it safe?

Step 4 — SIZE the work honestly:
- Confirm or revise the effort estimate from triage
- If the work is bigger than a single tech-debt item (touches >5 files, requires design decisions, or has user-visible impact), STOP and recommend promotion to a feature instead.

RECORD:
- One `decision`-type note titled "Resolution plan" containing: approach, affected files, blast radius, required pre-tests, sized effort.
- Additional `reference`-type notes pointing at the specific code locations you analyzed (file:line references are gold).
- `shark link {{.id}} <other-key> --type=related` for any new related entities you discover.
- If sizing exploded: `shark td note add {{.id}} --type=decision --content="Recommend cancel — scope exceeds tech debt. Promote to feature: <reasoning>."` then `shark status set {{.id}} cancelled --reason "Promoted to feature scope"`.

EXIT GATE:
- A `decision` note exists with a concrete resolution plan a developer can execute
- Risks and pre-tests are explicit
- Related links recorded
- {{template "advance" .}} (goes to `ready_for_resolution`)
